# OW data lookups go through `opticwise-postgres` first

OpticWise owns its data in the OWNet Postgres mirror. Any time the user asks
for **transcripts, emails, Slack messages, or files** (or any "show me…" /
"find…" query that maps to CRM-style data), the **first** tool you reach for
is the `opticwise-postgres` MCP. Other MCPs are fallbacks.

## Canonical tables (public schema, `opticwise_db`)

| Ask for… | Query these tables |
|---|---|
| Call transcripts / meetings | `ReadAIMeeting` (Read.ai is the only transcript source) |
| Emails (inbound/outbound, threads, templates) | `GmailMessage`, `EmailMessage`, `EmailThread`, `EmailTemplate` |
| Slack messages / sessions / users | `SlackMessageLog`, `SlackSession`, `SlackUser` |
| Files (Drive sync) | `DriveFile` |
| CRM context to join on | `User`, `Person`, `Organization`, `Deal`, `Activity`, `Note` |

Join on `personId` / `organizationId` / `dealId` / `syncUserId` when the user
asks "from X" or "about deal Y". `User.email` maps a person to their
`syncUserId` for Gmail and Slack tables.

## Fathom is deprecated — do not use

- **Ignore the `opticwise-fathom` MCP entirely.** It is removed from the
  active config. Do not call it, do not suggest it.
- **Ignore the `CallTranscript` and `CallTranscriptChunk` tables.** They are
  the legacy Fathom mirror (note the `fathomCallId` column on
  `CallTranscript`) and are not the source of truth for transcripts going
  forward.
- All meeting/transcript queries resolve from `ReadAIMeeting` (and its
  `transcript`, `transcriptJson`, `summary`, `actionItems`, `participants`,
  `topics`, `chapterSummaries` columns).

## Allowed fallbacks

Use a source-system MCP **only** when one of these is true:

1. The user explicitly asks to hit the live source (e.g. "pull from Gmail
   live", "open the Drive file").
2. The Postgres mirror returns **0 rows** for a category that should have
   data (e.g. `SlackMessageLog` is currently empty — say the data isn't
   there yet rather than substituting a different category).
3. You need a binary asset (PDF bytes, file contents) that isn't stored in
   Postgres — use `opticwise-gdrive` for the binary, but cite the row from
   Postgres for the record.

In every fallback, **say which MCP you used and why**. Never fall back to
Fathom.

## If `opticwise-postgres` is unavailable

If the project's `mcps/user-opticwise-postgres/` descriptor folder is missing
(MCP didn't start), do **not** silently route to Fathom/Drive/Gmail. Tell the
user the Postgres MCP isn't registered and recommend a Cursor restart or
toggling the MCP in **Settings → Tools & Integrations → MCP**.

## Rationale

> If you don't own your data & digital infrastructure, your vendors do.

The Postgres mirror is the owner-controlled layer. Source APIs (Gmail,
Slack, Drive, Read.ai) are vendor surfaces — use them only as taps into the
owned store.
